<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orange, Vaucluse</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arausio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Orange is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Roman colony of Arausio, founded for veterans around 35 BCE on the land of the Cavares. Its Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and triumphal arch are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of the Principality of Orange, which passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a> and gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> royal house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse.jpg" alt="Orange, Vaucluse" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeanlouiszimmermann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ORANGE-centreville_aerienne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Orange is a commune in the Vaucluse department of the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 21 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>. It lies on the departmental border with Gard, which follows the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>. The name derives from a Proto-Celtic form meaning in front of the ear and was originally unrelated to the orange fruit, with which it was later conflated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> form Arausio is attested in the first century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Celtic settlement named after a local sacred spring stood on the site. In 105 BCE a major battle was fought there between two Roman armies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutones">Teutones</a>. Roman Arausio covered about 70 hectares and was the capital of a wide area of northern Provence, with a monumental temple complex and a forum as well as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and arch. It was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in 412, had been a diocese since the end of the third century, and hosted synods in 441 and 529. The Second Council of Orange condemned what later came to be called Semipelagianism.</p><p>From the 12th century Orange was a minor principality, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>, count of Nassau, inherited the title Prince of Orange in 1544, the principality joined the holdings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a>, which drew it into the Protestant side of the Wars of Religion. The forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> captured it repeatedly in the late 17th century, and it was finally ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1713 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a>. The title stayed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> princes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Orange is known for its Roman architecture. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> is described as the most impressive still existing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the Triumphal Arch carries an inscription dedicated to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> in 27 CE; the arch, theatre and surroundings were listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1981. The museum displays the largest Roman cadastral maps ever recovered, etched on marble. The theatre, restored in 1869, hosts the annual Chorégies d'Orange opera festival.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Orange, Vaucluse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between <b>Arausio</b>, now Orange, and the Rhône, the Cimbri under Boiorix and the Teutons under Teutobod defeated two Roman armies under the proconsul Quintus Servilius Caepio and the consul Gnaeus Mallius Maximus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-6">6 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Their towns included Avennio (Avignon), Cabellio (Cavaillon) and <b>Arausio</b> (Orange), and they had close commercial ties with Massalia.</blockquote><a href="/state/cavares">Cavares</a></li></ul></section>
